<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Boyne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1690</span> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/williamite-war-in-ireland">Williamite War in Ireland</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Boyne was fought on 1 July 1690 (Old Style) on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-boyne">River Boyne</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drogheda">Drogheda</a>, Ireland, between the Protestant William III and the deposed Catholic king James II. William's victory forced James to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, though the war continued until 1691. It became a central symbol of Protestant ascendancy, commemorated by Ulster Protestants each 12 July.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-boyne.jpg" alt="Battle of the Boyne" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan van Huchtenburgh · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_slag_aan_de_Boyne_(Ierland)_tussen_Jacobus_II_en_Willem_III,_12_juli_1690_Rijksmuseum_SK-A-605_(cropped).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
